<!--
  Server settings UTMStack depends on, pinned rather than inherited.

  date_time_input_format decides whether a timestamp written as RFC3339 — with
  the T separator and a Z, which is what the pipeline produces — is accepted.
  Its default is not stable across ClickHouse releases: 25.3 ships "basic",
  which rejects those outright, and later versions ship "best_effort", which
  accepts them. Relying on the default means an insert that works on one
  version fails on another, and the failure is per-row at write time.

  The server version is pinned to 25.8 for a related reason: 25.3 cannot parse
  a nested object into a JSON column through JSONEachRow at all, which is how
  every log and alert is written.

  final makes a read of the alerts table return one row per alert rather than
  one per version of it. Alerts are stored in a ReplacingMergeTree because they
  are edited after they are written, and a ReplacingMergeTree only collapses
  versions when its parts happen to merge — until then a plain SELECT returns
  every version. Setting it here rather than per query is what keeps that true
  for every reader: the backend, the plugins, SOC-AI and anyone at a console.
  It applies only to engines that support FINAL, so logs and statistics, which
  are plain MergeTree, are unaffected.

  It is not free: collapsing happens per query, and what it costs grows with how
  many parts hold unmerged versions of the same alert. That number is bounded by
  how fast background merges keep up, so merge lag on utmstack.alerts is the
  thing to watch when alert queries slow down — not the query itself.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <date_time_input_format>best_effort</date_time_input_format>
            <final>1</final>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
